# mul_up_1 for the public building block kairn::mul_up (milestone2.md, "Rounding a product down
# and up"): both operands pass through guard, then mul_up_1 (rule R3, the factors of a fused
# operation: architecture.md). mul_up_1 only multiplies a and b, in the fms of its two_prod, which
# R3 alone does not guard; but a compiler that sees one of them to be 1 rewrites fms(a, 1, p) as
# a - p and fuses a product a = x y of the program into it, so that the sign of the error term that
# chooses the upper bound is that of the exact product (Apple clang 21 under -ffp-contract=fast,
# measured). After the guards each is a number of the format. A twin rather than a guard in
# mul_up_1: mul_dir_1 returns what mul_up_1 returns, a lemma of both models for every operand
# (DirectedProd.v, IeeeDirectedProd.v), which a guard in mul_up_1 would break for a NaN. Its
# claims are those of mul_up_1, in both models: the guard keeps the value of a finite number, an
# infinity, and a NaN a NaN. Not claimed: the sign of a zero result, which the guard can change.
kernel:        mul_up_entry
input:         a
input:         b
output:        r
value:         a*b
ensures:       bound r above steps=1
ensures:       rounded r up when=product_large
min_precision: 2
source:        mul_up_1 on its guarded operands (rule R3, the factors of a fused operation)
license:       algorithm and proof: MIT
theorem:       proofs/theories/EntryKernels.v mul_up_entry_correct
status:        proved
domain:        extended
ieee_status:   proved
ieee_theorem:  proofs/theories/IeeeEntry.v mul_up_entry_ieee_correct
entry:         yes
cone_exempt:   through mul_up_1, whose result is selected; the bound and rounded claims state the output for infinite and NaN operands

ga = guard(a)
gb = guard(b)
r  = mul_up_1(ga, gb)
